@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@
 3.19 (2021-11-20)
 -----------------
 
+- ``sqlite-utils insert`` now accepts a ``--convert`` option for transforming
+  each incoming JSON object using Python code before it is inserted.
 - The :ref:`table.lookup() method <python_api_lookup_tables>` now accepts keyword arguments that match those on the underlying ``table.insert()`` method: ``foreign_keys=``, ``column_order=``, ``not_null=``, ``defaults=``, ``extracts=``, ``conversions=`` and ``columns=``. You can also now pass ``pk=`` to specify a different column name to use for the primary key. (:issue:`342`)
 
 .. _v3_18:
diff --git a/docs/cli.rst b/docs/cli.rst
index a096fdeaa7887dacb1a4d37aa15f76c4236763e6..d8cc4aa1828a0b300a9aed9bfe2e098d989320de 100644
--- a/docs/cli.rst
+++ b/docs/cli.rst
@@ -676,6 +676,16 @@ Here's the simplest possible example::
 
     $ echo '{"name": "Cleo", "age": 4}' | sqlite-utils insert dogs.db dogs -
 
+You can transform each incoming JSON object using Python code passed to
+``--convert``. The object is available as ``row`` and should be modified in
+place. Every input object will still be inserted. For example::
+
+    $ echo '[{"n": "1"}, {"n": "2"}]' | \
+        sqlite-utils insert numbers.db numbers - \
+        --convert 'row["n"] = int(row["n"]) + 1'
+
+This inserts two rows where ``n`` is the integer value ``2`` and ``3``.
+
 To specify a column as the primary key, use ``--pk=column_name``.
 
 To create a compound primary key across more than one column, use ``--pk`` multiple times.
